# Template — copy into the TARGET repo's own .github/workflows/, not this one.
# See hooks/README.md for the full explanation and the two other trigger mechanisms.
#
# This template assumes a headless entry point that can run one skill's procedure
# non-interactively (e.g. `claude --print` given the scan-repo skill's prompt text,
# per the pattern the-professor-skill-suite-redesign.md's relationship note describes
# for #1402's own fresh-session verification, or an equivalent for whatever harness
# the target repo's maintainers use). No such entry point is built by this branch —
# fill in RUNNER_COMMAND once one exists.
#
# NOT SAFE TO DEPLOY AS-IS — RUNNER_COMMAND isn't filled in, and PROFESSOR_PACK_REF
# below must be pinned to a real tag/commit. Phase 6 (redesign doc §9) is where those
# get built, not this file.
#
# PROMPT-INJECTION THREAT MODEL — RESOLVED 2026-09-04 (redesign doc, Open Questions
# item 7). This job feeds a repo's own content back into an agent as drafting input —
# the classic indirect-injection shape. `verify-claims` and `screen-sensitive` (this
# pack's own gates) already narrow specific outcomes (a fabricated claim, an
# exfiltrated secret); CI re-verification and never-push-direct (below) mean nothing
# merges unreviewed. Neither covers the runner's OWN permission surface, so:
#
#   1. RUNNER_COMMAND must be scoped to exactly tools/professor.py's subcommands plus
#      read-only git/gh calls — no general shell access, no other network egress. This
#      is a real requirement on whatever fills RUNNER_COMMAND in, not a suggestion.
#   2. Mandatory human review of the PR this job opens is the accepted final backstop
#      — not a claim that injection is prevented, an explicit acceptance that layered
#      gates plus a human before merge is the posture, same as any unattended CI job
#      touching an LLM.
#
# CREDENTIAL DECISION — RESOLVED 2026-09-04 (redesign doc, Open Questions item 2): the
# default GITHUB_TOKEN below is the answer, not a placeholder waiting on a PAT/GitHub
# App. This holds because this template runs in the SAME repo it targets (you copy it
# into your own .github/workflows/) — GITHUB_TOKEN is auto-issued per run, scoped only
# to this repo, and needs no secret to create or rotate. A separately provisioned
# credential is only for a different shape (one place scanning many repos it doesn't
# host workflows in), which this template does not attempt.
#
# ONE REAL SETTING YOU MUST CHECK, THAT THIS FILE CANNOT SET FOR YOU: this repo's own
# Settings -> Actions -> General -> Workflow permissions must have "Allow GitHub
# Actions to create and approve pull requests" checked. That toggle is separate from
# the read/write default below and from the `permissions:` block — without it,
# create-pull-request fails even though everything here looks correctly scoped.

name: professor-scan

on:
  schedule:
    # Daily by default — capability 5's "daily (or configurable-interval)". Change
    # the cron expression to whatever interval this repo's maintainers want; nothing
    # else in this workflow depends on the specific value.
    - cron: "0 6 * * *"
  workflow_dispatch: {}   # manual trigger, for testing the workflow itself

# TWO JOBS, DELIBERATELY — RESOLVED 2026-09-05 (redesign doc §7.1, corrected after a
# real gap a review found: this document used to claim CI re-verification was "a
# separate job" while this template ran it as a later step in the SAME job, sharing
# the same checkout and credentials as the drafting run). Real independence needs a
# real boundary:
#   - `scan-and-draft` never gets write access at all (no `contents`/`pull-requests`
#     permission) — it only reads the target repo, runs the drafting agent over its
#     content, and uploads a patch of the result as a build artifact. If prompt
#     injection compromises this job, it has nothing to write with.
#   - `verify-and-open-pr` never runs the drafting agent and never processes raw
#     target-repo content as agent input. It downloads the patch, applies it to a
#     FRESH checkout (not the one the drafting job touched), independently re-runs
#     all three gates against every file the patch touches — modified AND newly
#     added, `git apply --index` stages both — and only then, if everything passes,
#     opens the PR with the write-scoped credential this job alone holds.
# This also fixes a second bug the same review found: the old single-job version's
# re-verification loop used `git diff --name-only HEAD`, which only lists modified
# TRACKED files — it silently skipped every newly-drafted page, draft-page's actual
# output. Re-deriving the changed-file list from the applied patch (below) doesn't
# have that gap: a freshly-applied patch's files are staged, tracked or not.
permissions:
  contents: read   # top-level default; each job below states its own, narrower need

env:
  # Pin this to a real tag or commit SHA before this template is ever actually used —
  # an unpinned branch means every run executes whatever the pack's default branch
  # currently holds, not a version anyone reviewed for this target repo.
  PROFESSOR_PACK_REF: "REPLACE-WITH-A-PINNED-TAG-OR-COMMIT-SHA"
  # One variable, used by every step that needs to find the pack — never a bare
  # `tools/professor.py` assumption, which silently breaks the moment the checkout
  # path below changes. `${{ github.workspace }}` is the target repo's own checkout
  # root; the pack lands one level under the sibling `.professor-pack` checkout.
  PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT: "${{ github.workspace }}/.professor-pack/launchpad/agents/the-professor"

jobs:
  scan-and-draft:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read   # never write access — see this file's header for why
    steps:
      - name: Check out this repo
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0   # scan-repo's change detection needs real git history,
                            # not a shallow clone — a shallow checkout would make
                            # every path look "changed since the beginning of time"
          persist-credentials: false   # this job never pushes anything; don't leave
                                        # a credential configured that it doesn't need

      - name: Check out The Professor's skill pack, pinned
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          repository: launchpad-26/buzz
          ref: ${{ env.PROFESSOR_PACK_REF }}
          path: .professor-pack
          sparse-checkout: |
            launchpad/agents/the-professor
          persist-credentials: false

      - name: Run scan-repo, then draft-page/update-page for every gap, then library-index sweep and provenance-log archive
        env:
          PROFESSOR_TARGET: ${{ github.workspace }}
        run: |
          # RUNNER_COMMAND — fill in once a headless skill-runner exists. Must be
          # scoped to exactly tools/professor.py's subcommands plus read-only git/gh
          # calls — no general shell access, no other network egress (see this file's
          # header, "PROMPT-INJECTION THREAT MODEL" — this is Phase 6's own required
          # part 1, not optional hardening to add later). This job holds no write
          # credential at all (see this file's header, "TWO JOBS, DELIBERATELY") —
          # that's the primary defense, this scoping requirement is the second layer,
          # not the only one. Must:
          #   1. run scan-repo's procedure against $PROFESSOR_TARGET
          #   2. for each `new` entry, run draft-page; for each `stale` entry
          #      (including every needs_external_check one), run update-page — both
          #      already end at screen-sensitive before any write
          #   3. run library-index in sweep mode once, regardless of whether step 2
          #      drafted or updated anything — Phase 6 (redesign doc §9) claims this
          #      workflow exercises "library health," and that's only true if sweep
          #      actually runs every time, not only alongside a content change
          #   4. hand every `removed` entry from step 1 to library-index for
          #      reconciliation, per scan-repo's own §5 hand-off
          #   5. run provenance-log in archive mode for every page in the library,
          #      also unconditionally — the archive threshold (default 365 days) is
          #      the reason this runs on a schedule at all, not only when content
          #      changes; a run with nothing to draft/update should still archive
          #   6. leave every write on the local checkout uncommitted — this job never
          #      commits or pushes; the next job applies a patch instead
          #   7. exit non-zero if any gate (check-page/screen-content) reported a
          #      `block` it could not resolve, or if any step above failed outright —
          #      the next step must not run on a non-zero exit here
          #   Use "$PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT/tools/professor.py" for every gate/subcommand
          #   call — never a bare "tools/professor.py", which assumes the pack lives
          #   inside the target repo's own checkout, which it doesn't (see the pack
          #   checkout step above; $PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT is set once, in this
          #   workflow's top-level `env:`, for every step to share).
          echo "RUNNER_COMMAND placeholder — see this file's header comment" >&2
          exit 1

      - name: Turn every change into a patch, excluding the pack checkout
        if: ${{ success() }}
        run: |
          # Stage everything the drafting run touched — modified AND newly created
          # pages both, via -A — except the pack checkout itself, which is never
          # part of the TARGET repo's own diff. `git diff --cached` after this
          # correctly includes untracked new files, unlike a plain `git diff HEAD`
          # (the bug a review found in this template's own prior version).
          git add -A -- . ':(exclude).professor-pack'
          git diff --cached --binary > "${{ runner.temp }}/professor-changes.patch"
          # Empty patch is a valid outcome (nothing to draft/update this run) —
          # the next job's own steps are all conditioned on the patch having content.

      - name: Upload the patch for the verification job to apply independently
        if: ${{ success() }}
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: professor-changes
          path: ${{ runner.temp }}/professor-changes.patch
          retention-days: 1

  verify-and-open-pr:
    needs: scan-and-draft
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write         # the only job with write access at all — see this
                               # file's header, "TWO JOBS, DELIBERATELY"
      pull-requests: write    # also requires this repo's own "Allow GitHub Actions
                               # to create and approve pull requests" setting (see
                               # file header)
    steps:
      - name: Check out this repo, fresh — not the checkout scan-and-draft used
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          persist-credentials: false   # create-pull-request gets its own token
                                        # explicitly, below — don't also leave one
                                        # sitting in git config for this checkout

      - name: Check out The Professor's skill pack, pinned — also fresh
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          repository: launchpad-26/buzz
          ref: ${{ env.PROFESSOR_PACK_REF }}
          path: .professor-pack
          sparse-checkout: |
            launchpad/agents/the-professor
          persist-credentials: false

      - name: Download the patch the drafting job produced
        uses: actions/download-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: professor-changes
          path: ${{ runner.temp }}

      - name: Stop here if there was nothing to draft or update this run
        id: has-changes
        run: |
          if [ -s "${{ runner.temp }}/professor-changes.patch" ]; then
            echo "changed=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          else
            echo "changed=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          fi

      - name: Apply the patch to this fresh checkout
        if: steps.has-changes.outputs.changed == 'true'
        run: git apply --index "${{ runner.temp }}/professor-changes.patch"

      - name: Independently re-verify every changed page against all three gates
        if: steps.has-changes.outputs.changed == 'true'
        run: |
          # This step exists because "unskippable" (screen-sensitive's and
          # verify-claims' own SKILL.md files both say this) is a prompt instruction
          # to the drafting agent, not an enforced boundary — an agent under time
          # pressure could still write a page without running a gate, and nothing
          # upstream of this step would catch that. CI is the actual boundary (the
          # original design's own "enforce at the boundary where the violation
          # happens" rule) — this is this workflow's own enforcement of the
          # suite-wide "every gate runs twice" rule (redesign doc §6's flow-diagram
          # note, decided 2026-09-04): re-run check-page, screen-content, AND
          # verify-claims here, independently, against every page the applied patch
          # touches, and fail the job if any of the three reports a finding —
          # regardless of what the drafting job already claimed to have checked. This
          # job runs in a completely separate checkout with no memory of the
          # drafting job's runtime state, so "independent" is real here, not just a
          # separate step in the same mutable job (the gap a review found in this
          # template's own prior version — see this file's header).
          #
          # `git diff --cached --name-only` after `git apply --index` (previous step)
          # correctly lists every file the patch touched, modified or newly added —
          # `git apply --index` stages new files too, so this doesn't have the
          # "misses untracked files" bug the old single-job version's `git diff
          # --name-only HEAD` had.
          # FILL IN once tools/professor.py exists (redesign doc §9, Phase 1):
          #   git diff --cached --name-only -z -- '*.md' | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
          #     "$PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT/tools/professor.py" check-page "$f" --target . || exit 1
          #     "$PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT/tools/professor.py" screen-content "$f" --target . || exit 1
          #   done
          # verify-claims' dispatch — RESOLVED (redesign doc, Open Questions item 9):
          # it runs $PROFESSOR_VERIFIER_CMD as a subprocess (same override pattern as
          # PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT, INCLUDING no suite-applied default — this variable
          # must be set in this job's own env; `claude --print` is the recommended
          # value, not a fallback applied for you), fed only a claim's cited span and
          # sentence, per claim, per changed page. Deliberately NOT a
          # tools/professor.py subcommand (redesign doc §4's diagram note) — this is
          # verify-claims' own dispatch logic, kept separate from the toolkit's four
          # deterministic subcommands because this one dispatches a model, not a
          # script. FILL IN once that dispatch logic exists (Phase 1b):
          #   git diff --cached --name-only -z -- '*.md' | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
          #     <run verify-claims' own procedure against "$f", which itself calls
          #      $PROFESSOR_VERIFIER_CMD once per claim> || exit 1
          #   done
          # Fail this job if any claim's verdict is anything other than SUPPORTED.
          echo "independent re-verification placeholder — see this step's comment" >&2
          exit 1

      - name: Remove the pack checkout before anything inspects the diff
        if: ${{ success() && steps.has-changes.outputs.changed == 'true' }}
        run: |
          # $PROFESSOR_PACK_ROOT's checkout (the "Check out The Professor's skill
          # pack" step, above) lives inside this job's own workspace, sibling to the
          # target repo's checkout, which means it's inside the tree
          # create-pull-request scans for changes by default. Nothing about the pack
          # itself should ever appear in a PR against the TARGET repo — remove it
          # before the PR step runs, don't rely on that step's own path filtering to
          # catch it.
          rm -rf "${{ github.workspace }}/.professor-pack"

      - name: Open a PR with anything drafted or updated
        if: ${{ success() && steps.has-changes.outputs.changed == 'true' }}
                                 # never on a prior failure — a blocked gate or a
                                 # failed step must not still produce a PR;
                                 # `!cancelled()` (a prior version of this template)
                                 # is the wrong condition, since it runs on failure
                                 # too and only skips on cancellation
        uses: peter-evans/create-pull-request@v6
        with:
          token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}   # explicit — this checkout deliberately
                                                # set persist-credentials: false above
          commit-message: "docs: professor scheduled scan"
          branch: professor/scheduled-scan
          title: "The Professor: scheduled documentation scan"
          body: |
            Automated run of `scan-repo`, `draft-page`/`update-page`, and a
            `library-index` sweep, on the schedule configured in this workflow. Every
            change here passed `check-page`, `screen-sensitive`, and `verify-claims`
            during drafting, in a separate job with no write access, and was
            independently re-verified against all three gates again in this job, in
            a completely fresh checkout — not just trusted from the drafting run.
            This PR is the review point the gate hands off to, per the original
            design's own "the enforcement
            boundary decides where output goes" rule: nothing here merges to the
            default branch without this pull request being reviewed.

            <!-- RUNNER_COMMAND should append, per updated page, a line naming the
            code change each update actually responds to — pulled straight from the
            sources this run's own provenance-log calls already resolved (redesign
            doc §8), not a second lookup:
              - docs/relay/tenant-scoping.md: crates/buzz-relay/src/tenant.rs changed
                in #1987 (Jane Dev, 2026-08-30)
            A reviewer approving a docs-only PR should be able to see *why* it
            exists without opening a second tab to check what changed and who
            changed it. -->
          # Nothing pushes directly to the default branch — the gate's guarantee
          # only reaches a pull request, same reasoning as
          # the-professor-design.md §8, "Also unspecified: where drafts go."
